A||||16||loafer87gt||Why does the stock v6  spin bearings?||||1||
Z||000000||loafer87gt||08-09-2000||01:48 AM||||It seems like this is a common problem with the stock 2.8. Why is this? Is it because of impoper oiling or is the engine not properly balanced from the factory. I am in the process of getting my engine rebuilt and I am wondering what I could do to prevent this from happening again.||||reg||1
Z||000001||DJRice||08-09-2000||08:54 AM||||Bearings get 'spun' because of improper lubrication.  The best way to get the maximum life out of your engine is to change oil every 3 months/3000 miles.  By keeping fresh oil and a clean filter in your engine, you greatly reduce the chances of your oil system getting clogged.  <P>A teflon coat (PTFE, Slick50, etc.) wouldnt hurt, but its not really necessary.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Taijiguy||08-09-2000||09:14 AM||||I don't know what makes them spin initially, I'm sure each caase is different. Although, I have to wonder if prolonged hard cornering doesn't have it's effect. I spun the number five and six bearings in my GT a couple of years ago. Then it turned out that I got a "custom"   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> " set of main bearings. (The thrust bearing lacked an oiling oil) Not having benefit of this forum at that time, I didn't realize it was wrong) I inspected the crank, and figured maybe the thrust got it's oil from the others via the crankshaft. I checked every available reference, but nothing. I figured it must be OK, the bearings were brand new, stilll in the shrink wrap. Hell, I didn't know...I thought about drilling an oil hole, but then was afraid I'd affect the pressure and cause a problem elsewhere.  Spun the same two bearings the very second it fired for the first time after the rebuild. I still managed to drive it like that for almost two years before deciding to rebuild it again. (Turned out a got a set with two lower thrust bearings   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> )<BR>Here's the most valuable lessons I've learned about making sure bearings stay intact, in Fiero's and others:<P>Make sure your bearings are the right size, and have all the right oiling holes!<P>Make sure when rebuilding, that you properly reinstall the caps- both rod and main. Why is this important? Because the bolck is line bored with the caps on, which means it should be close, but the bore may not be EXACTLY in the center. So when you flip a cap and install it backwards, you bind the crank since the sections of the opening aren't aligned.<P>Use a HIGH QUALITY assembly lube, it's more expensive, but well worth it in my book.<P>Install a high pressure high volume oil pump. Pretty self explanitory<P>I installed a Moroso oil accumulator. During sustained hard cornering, if the oil sloshes away from the pump pickup. the accumulator will pick up the slack for a few seconds. The next best option would be a dry sump setup.<P>If you already have bearings that are spun, you can drop the pan and replace the bearings from underneath. Smooth out the crank journals with emery cloth followed by 1000 grit wetordry sandpaper. Mic the crank journal in a number of different positions and locations to check for taper, roundness, and size. Plaastigage the new bearings, and if you're within specs, all is golden!<P>Whew, I didn't realize how long this had gotten. Sorry about the lenght, I hope there's SOME useable information in here someplace.....<P><BR>-Taiji<P>(never at a loss for words)<p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 08-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gold-86SE||08-09-2000||09:46 AM||||<BR>Okay, you guys have me at a loss.<P>What are you talking about, spinning the bearings? And, how do you know if it has happened?<BR><P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||08-09-2000||09:57 AM||||The bearings that attach between the cranckshaft and the block. Also known as Main Bearings. If the bearings don't get oil the crankshaft will "grab" the bearing which will now spin within the block. You'll know when it happens the bearing spinning sounds like a wire brush on metal. Since bearings are of a softer material than a block or crankshaft they tend to grind away then you have a knock from the engine. Also usually during the grinding process the crank and block can get scored which requires repair before replacing the bearing.<BR>As for 2.8L being notorious for spinning bearings I always thought it was the 2.8L in the trucks (s10,s15,blazer, and jimmy)not the FWD 2.8's. I have heard that in hard cornering the Fiero 2.8 can get oil starved.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 08-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||loafer87gt||08-09-2000||10:06 AM||||I read in one of the issues of GM high performace about a thing called a windage tray that is supposed to help with oiling at high RPMS. Is there such a thing available for our v6? Also, how much is a high volume oil pump, and I would I be able to order this from a place like summit? Thanks guys, good info here<P>Jason<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||08-09-2000||11:48 AM||||During the development of the 660 engine, GM discovered a problem with failing rod bearings, primarily #4. They solved it with the HVHP oil pump (used in the code Z engine original and later in the code 9 engines) but was never able to figure out why it was happening. <P>I alway run the HVHP pump and the blue stripe spring. Solid 70PSI except when it is very hot and idling. I also run syth oil. I have over 250k on three different 660's and they survived. Timing chains get real loose!<P>After that it psychic phenomenon that make them fail. Murphy's Law, etc.||||reg||1
Z||000007||1FST2M6||08-09-2000||11:56 AM||||I wiped out #5. I RAILED on that motor for 8 months with 16+ bottles of nitrous. took the blue bottle off the car, then 3+ months later its toast. 6 bucks later, 5 hours under the car and POOF no knock.... 1300miles later the knock came back... time for a new engine. all the other bearings are fine... ||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||08-09-2000||12:39 PM||||One common thing to do with oil starving is install a dam in the pan. This is a wall at the top of the sump with a little on way door in it. It prevents oil climbing out of the sump. This can be done in a number of ways.<P>Another is to simply baffle the top of the sump leaving a big enough hole to drop the pump and pickup thru. This works best if the pickup is below, or close to, the rear main.<P>A third, wich may not be vailable for this V6, is to use a swinging pickup. This is often done in combination with one of the above.<P>All these methods can be used on both inline and transverse setups.<P>A  simple windage try will not help with the oil starving problem much if at all. However some windage trays also have a dam that hangs down. While not as good as a dam mounted on the pad it doesn't require fabrication or welding/brasing.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Taijiguy||08-09-2000||12:47 PM||||I'm going to be careful here not to step on any toes, but I want to expound on a comment by Oreif, in that a spun bearing CAN be a potentially costly experience. The bearing can definitely cause extensive damage to the crank, necessitating having the crank ground, and requiring oversize bearings. HOWEVER, in my case for example, the damage to the crank was minimal. I was able to smooth the journal and reuse it without having it ground. (I was rebuilding my engine anyway, but the service of a bearing can be done with the motor in place if no machine work is required) The key is to completely smooth out the crank. Minor radial scoring souldn't be a problem, as long as you can't really feel them with your finger, or snag a fingernail on them. It's also IMPERITIVE that you verify the journals' shape by micing as I mentioned in my earlier post. It has to be ROUND, The cross section must be FLAT, and it must have a limited amount of TAPER. If any of those are off, you are officially screwed, and  have to pull it and have it machined. (Chiltons provides specs for those tolerances)<BR>The Fiero 2.8 has a windage tray that is integral to the oil pan. It's purpose is to prevent oil from being splashed up onto the crank (by the "wind" created by the turning of the crank, thus the name) Supposedly oil splashing on the rotating assembly can eat up a few horses. <P>Cripes, another long post. You can tell I've been down this road once or twice huh? <P>-Taiji<P>(still got stuff to say!)   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||Will||08-09-2000||01:35 PM||||Oil pressure is an oft misunderstood topic. A street engine does not need 80 psi of oil pressure.<BR>     Here's why: The crankshaft has small passages in it that allow oil to flow inside the crank. The oil enters at the rear main cap and flows through the passages inside the crank, reappearing in the other mains and in the rod bearings.<BR>     The engine needs ONLY enough oil pressure to overcome centrifugal force and push the oil inward from the rod journal to the main journal inside the spinning crank.<BR>     Any oil pressure in excess of this is unecessary and wasteful. Building up extra pressure heats the oil unecessarily by squeezing it through small orifices against its viscosity. Excess oil pressure also makes the oil pump a larger parasitic loss the engine must overcome.<BR>     Race engines need high oil pressure because they spin very fast and have much more centrifugal force to overcome.<BR>     If I were to build a GM 60 degree V6, I wold definitely put a high volume pump in it, as I helped a friend of mine do to his 60k mile engine last weekend. The extra oil volume helps fill up clearances that have opened from wear. This is especially important in a 2.8, because it was not adequately lubricated from the factory.<BR>     But I would use a regulator spring that allowed only enough pressure to overcome centrifugal force at 6000 rpm.<BR>     Whe I helped my friend put his new pump in, we reused the factory original pressure regulator spring, even though a stiffer one came with the pump. The factory spring in the new pump "only" makes 60-70 psi, but that is MORE than enough.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Will||08-09-2000||01:46 PM||||To answer the question originally asked:<BR>Why does the 2.8 spin bearings?<P>1. GM has never been able to make a long lived 60 degree V6. At least not as long lived as their V8's and 90 degree V6's (derived from V8's).<P>2. The 2.8, GM's first 60 degree V6, is the worst of the pack. The oil galleries were not as well designed as the ones in GM V8's. Also, the engine did not have enough oil pump capacity. And in Fieros, the oil pan capacity had to be reduced so that the pan could clear the exhaust pipe.<P>3. Abuse. The 2.8 was never meant to be a hard ridden engine like 327 & 350 small blocks, 427 side oiling Fords, or a Can-Am 430's.<BR>   However, from what I have seen, many who frequent this forum believe that it was designed for high-po use. The engine is used beyond its capacity and consequently breaks at the weakest point: the poorly lubricated main or rod bearings.<P>2.8's should not be spun over 4000 rpm if there is more than 100k on the engine.<BR>I spun a rod bearing on an (admittedly well aged) 2.8 by doing just that.<BR>   A small block V8 doesn't need this ginger treatment until it gets over 200k. A big block is in the range of like 250-300k.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Carrolles||08-09-2000||03:28 PM||||I have never heard that Fiero V-6s have a problem with spun bearings.  No one I know in the Fiero community that I know (up until now) has ever mentioned a problem with this.<P>Our engines are prone to building up 'coked' oil in the return valleys of the intake manifold due to the higher temperatures these engines typically run.  I found this to be the case on mu 85SE v-6 with 120000 miles on it.  I caught it before it caused problems with oil starvation down below.||||reg||1
Z||000013||TheFiero64||08-09-2000||06:32 PM||||I bought my 86 GT off of ebay for 1000 because it had a spun bearing. The guy came up to deliver it and wanted to try out my 88 coupe. I said only if I go for the ride too. I got in and he brought it to 5 grand! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> . I yelled and said I never bring it over 4 grand and you will not either!!! The next day I noticed the oil pressure was lower and I changed the oil with no improvement. Then came the rod knock. I now know how my GT got the bearing spun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">. So now I have the v6 in my 88 that was meant for my GT. I am saving up for something good to put in my GT now. Maybe a v8  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||trigger||08-09-2000||07:27 PM||||The fiero store sells a windage tray for the V-6.<P>------------------<BR>Honestly officer, I was just keeping up with the flow of traffic.  I know there aren't any cars in the area, that's how far behind I am.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Taijiguy||08-09-2000||07:45 PM||||You don't need to buy a windage tray for the 2.8, it's integral to the oil pan on the Fiero.<P>-Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000016||PJ||08-09-2000||08:13 PM||||Good discussion going on here. I've always used the OEM main bearings as I can't find an aftermarket set with clearances (.0025") that I like. I think at some point GM revised the recommended clearances (and the bearings)to a smaller number. I have run the car for up to an hour and a half spinning the motor between 4 & 6 grand with no oil related failures. Will is right. I believe the high pressure pump I have installed contributes to the oil temp being way high (about 280*, but hey, I run Mobil 1). Any excess pressure robs HP and adds heat. I'm working on this but it becomes complicated when you have 24 feet of braided hose ($) running to the oil cooler in front of the car and back. The slosh shield in the oil pan isn't really a windage tray but it does do a good job keeping the pickup covered. For a nice OEM windage tray look up the one GM used in the '93 A cars with the 3.1L. Fits tight against the crank and can't cost much. You gotta get the studded main bolts to go with it though.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black<BR>  ||||reg||1
